<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8">
  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
  <meta name="theme-color" content="#0a0a0f">
  <title>Terms of Service · <%= siteName %></title>
  <link rel="icon" href="<%= siteLogo %>" type="image/png">
  <style>
    :root {
      --bg: #0a0a0f;
      --border: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.09);
      --text: #e6e6ef;
      --muted: #9a9aad;
      --accent: #6366f1;
    }
    * { box-sizing: border-box; }
    body {
      margin: 0;
      background: var(--bg);
      color: var(--text);
      font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, "Segoe UI", Roboto, Helvetica, Arial, sans-serif;
      font-size: 15px;
      line-height: 1.7;
    }
    .wrap { max-width: 800px; margin: 0 auto; padding: 56px 24px 96px; }
    header { margin-bottom: 40px; padding-bottom: 24px; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border); }
    header .brand {
      display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 10px;
      font-size: 13px; color: var(--muted); margin-bottom: 18px; text-decoration: none;
    }
    header .brand img { width: 22px; height: 22px; border-radius: 5px; }
    h1 { font-size: 30px; margin: 0 0 8px; letter-spacing: -0.4px; }
    .updated { font-size: 13px; color: var(--muted); }
    h2 {
      font-size: 18px; margin: 38px 0 10px; padding-top: 20px;
      border-top: 1px solid var(--border); letter-spacing: -0.2px;
    }
    h2:first-of-type { border-top: none; padding-top: 0; }
    p, li { color: #cfcfdd; }
    ul, ol { padding-left: 22px; }
    li { margin-bottom: 7px; }
    a { color: #8b8dfa; }
    table { width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; margin: 16px 0; font-size: 13.5px; }
    th, td { text-align: left; padding: 9px 10px; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border); vertical-align: top; }
    th { color: var(--muted); font-weight: 600; font-size: 12px; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: 0.5px; }
    code {
      background: rgba(255,255,255,0.07); padding: 1px 6px; border-radius: 4px;
      font-size: 13px; font-family: ui-monospace, SFMono-Regular, Menlo, monospace;
    }
    .note {
      background: rgba(99, 102, 241, 0.08); border-left: 3px solid var(--accent);
      padding: 14px 16px; border-radius: 6px; margin: 18px 0; font-size: 14px;
    }
    .warn {
      background: rgba(245, 158, 11, 0.08); border-left: 3px solid #f59e0b;
      padding: 14px 16px; border-radius: 6px; margin: 18px 0; font-size: 14px;
    }
    .banned { border: 1px solid rgba(239,68,68,0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 4px 18px 10px; margin: 14px 0; }
    .banned h3 { color: #ef8b8b; font-size: 14px; margin: 14px 0 6px; }
    footer {
      margin-top: 56px; padding-top: 24px; border-top: 1px solid var(--border);
      font-size: 12.5px; color: var(--muted); display: flex; gap: 16px; flex-wrap: wrap;
    }
    footer a { color: var(--muted); }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
<div class="wrap">

  <header>
    <a class="brand" href="/login">
      <img src="<%= siteLogo %>" alt="">
      <span><%= siteName %></span>
    </a>
    <h1>Terms of Service</h1>
    <div class="updated">
      Last updated: <%= updated %>
      <% if (companyName) { %> · Operated by <%= companyName %><% } %>
    </div>
  </header>

  <p>
    These terms govern your use of <strong><%= siteName %></strong> and the virtual
    machines, containers and related services provided through it. By creating an
    account, placing an order, or using a service, you agree to them.
  </p>
  <p>
    Read them together with the <a href="/privacy">Privacy Policy</a>, which explains
    what data is collected and why.
  </p>

  <% if (!companyName) { %>
  <div class="warn">
    The operator of this panel has not configured their business details yet.
    They must be set before these terms are shown to customers —
    <strong>Admin &rarr; Settings &rarr; Privacy</strong>.
  </div>
  <% } %>

  <h2>1. Who you are contracting with</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Operator:</strong> <%= companyName || siteName %></li>
    <% if (contactAddress) { %><li><strong>Address:</strong> <%= contactAddress %></li><% } %>
    <% if (contactEmail) { %><li><strong>Contact:</strong> <a href="mailto:<%= contactEmail %>"><%= contactEmail %></a></li><% } %>
    <% if (abuseEmail) { %><li><strong>Abuse reports:</strong> <a href="mailto:<%= abuseEmail %>"><%= abuseEmail %></a></li><% } %>
  </ul>
  <p>
    If you are a consumer, you are the natural person acting for purposes outside your
    trade or profession. If you are acting for a business, you confirm you are authorised
    to bind it to these terms.
  </p>

  <h2>2. Your account</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>You must give accurate details and keep them current.</li>
    <li>You are responsible for everything done through your account. Keep your password
      and any two-factor device secure, and tell the operator immediately if you believe
      someone else has access.</li>
    <li>One account per person or organisation. Accounts are not transferable without
      the operator's agreement.</li>
    <li>You must be old enough to enter a binding contract where you live. If you are a
      minor, you need a guardian's consent.</li>
    <li>The operator may suspend or close an account for a serious breach of these terms,
      for non-payment, or where required by law. Where it is reasonable to do so, you will
      be told why and given a chance to put things right.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>3. What is provided</h2>
  <p>
    The panel lets you create and manage virtual machines and containers on
    QEMU/KVM hosts, together with the surrounding infrastructure:
  </p>
  <table>
    <tr><th>Capability</th><th>What it means</th></tr>
    <tr><td>Virtual machines</td><td>KVM instances with configurable CPU, memory, disk and networking</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Containers</td><td>LXC containers from the available templates</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Storage</td><td>Volumes, snapshots and scheduled backups</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Networking</td><td>IP allocations, port forwarding and firewall rules</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Monitoring</td><td>Resource metrics and alert rules</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Multiple nodes</td><td>Machines spread across connected compute nodes</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Reselling</td><td>Where enabled, offering services to your own customers</td></tr>
  </table>
  <p>
    Availability of any particular template, operating system image or feature depends on
    what the operator has configured. The operator may change or withdraw features, and
    will give reasonable notice where a change materially affects a paid service.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>The operator supplies infrastructure, not content.</strong> What runs inside
    your machines is yours to install, configure, secure and support.
  </p>

  <h2>4. Prices, billing and renewal</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Prices are shown before you order and are in the currency displayed at checkout.</li>
    <li>Services run for the period you selected. Unless you cancel before it ends, a
      service may renew for a further period and the price for that period will be charged.</li>
    <li>Invoices and receipts are available in the panel. Keep your billing details current.</li>
    <li>If a payment fails, the operator may suspend the affected service after giving
      notice. Reactivation may depend on payment of the outstanding amount.</li>
    <li>Account credit is generally not refundable in cash, except where the law requires
      it.</li>
    <li>Prices may change with reasonable notice. If you are a consumer and do not accept
      a change that affects a service you already pay for, you may cancel it.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>5. Cancellation and refunds</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>You can cancel a service from the panel at any time; it runs until the end of the
      period you have already paid for.</li>
    <li>Deleting a service destroys its data. Take a backup first — deletion cannot be
      undone.</li>
    <li><strong>Consumers in the EU/UK have a right of withdrawal</strong> for distance
      contracts. For digital services it ends early once performance has begun with your
      express consent and your acknowledgement that you lose that right. Because a virtual
      machine is provisioned within minutes of ordering, that is normally the case. Ask the
      operator before ordering if you are unsure.</li>
    <li>Where a statutory refund right applies, it is not affected by anything in these terms.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>6. Acceptable use</h2>
  <p>
    Use a service only for lawful purposes and in a way that does not damage the service,
    the operator's infrastructure, or anyone else's systems. This section applies to you
    and to anyone you allow to use your services.
  </p>

  <div class="banned">
    <h3>Never, on any service</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Illegal content</strong> — including material that sexualises minors,
        incites violence or hatred, or promotes terrorism or extremism.</li>
      <li><strong>Infringement</strong> — distributing copyrighted works you have no right
        to, or violating trademarks or other rights.</li>
      <li><strong>Fraud and deception</strong> — phishing, scams, identity theft, forged
        documents, or fake payment instruments.</li>
      <li><strong>Malware</strong> — viruses, trojans, ransomware, botnet controllers or
        command-and-control infrastructure.</li>
      <li><strong>Attacks on others</strong> — denial-of-service, port scanning, brute
        forcing, unauthorised access to systems you do not own.</li>
      <li><strong>Spam</strong> — unsolicited bulk email, open mail relays, or services
        built to bypass spam filters.</li>
      <li><strong>Cryptocurrency mining</strong> — mining coins is not permitted, and neither
        is any comparable workload that exists only to consume CPU or GPU cycles for coin
        generation.</li>
      <li><strong>Sanctions violations</strong> — use by, for, or on behalf of anyone on an
        applicable sanctions list, or in an embargoed territory.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <p>
    High-risk activity — unlicensed gambling or financial services, anonymising proxies
    used to conceal unlawful activity, or anything that endangers network stability —
    needs the operator's written agreement first. Ask; do not assume.
  </p>

  <h2>7. Security and your responsibilities</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Keep your machines patched. You have administrative control, so you own their
      security.</li>
    <li>Do not endanger the network, the shared hardware, or other customers' services
      through excessive load or misconfiguration.</li>
    <li>Take your own backups unless a backup service is explicitly part of what you
      bought. Scheduled backups inside the panel are a convenience, not a guarantee.</li>
    <li>Where you resell services, you must bind your own customers to terms at least as
      protective as these, and you remain responsible to the operator for their conduct.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>8. Suspension and termination</h2>
  <p>
    If these terms are broken, the operator may, in proportion to the seriousness of the
    problem: warn you and ask you to fix it; remove or block specific content; suspend the
    affected service; or terminate the contract for serious breach. Where the breach
    endangers infrastructure or involves clearly unlawful content, a service may be
    suspended without prior notice. Information may be disclosed to authorities where the
    law requires it.
  </p>
  <p>
    You may close your account at any time, provided no services are active and no amount
    is outstanding. Deleting an account removes your services and their data.
  </p>

  <h2>9. Abuse reports</h2>
  <p>
    <% if (abuseEmail) { %>
      Report abuse or unlawful content to
      <a href="mailto:<%= abuseEmail %>"><%= abuseEmail %></a>.
    <% } else { %>
      Report abuse or unlawful content to the operator using the contact address above.
    <% } %>
    Reports should identify the service and the specific problem. The operator will review
    them and act where warranted, and will inform the parties concerned of the outcome
    where it lawfully can.
  </p>

  <h2>10. Data protection</h2>
  <p>
    The operator processes personal data as described in the
    <a href="/privacy">Privacy Policy</a>. Where you store other people's personal data on
    your machines, you are the controller of that data and the operator acts on your
    instructions. Ask the operator if you need a data processing agreement under
    Article 28 GDPR.
  </p>

  <h2>11. Liability</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Liability is unlimited for death or personal injury, for intentional wrongdoing,
      for gross negligence, and for anything else the law does not allow to be limited.</li>
    <li>For ordinary negligence, the operator is liable only for breach of a core
      contractual duty, and then only for loss that is typical and foreseeable for this
      kind of service.</li>
    <li>Beyond that, liability for ordinary negligence is excluded.</li>
    <li>Liability under mandatory product liability law, or under an expressly given
      guarantee, is unaffected.</li>
    <li>The operator is not liable for outages caused by events outside their control,
      including force majeure, upstream provider failures, or attacks on the network.</li>
    <li>You are responsible for the content you publish. The operator does not review it
      in advance and takes no responsibility for it.</li>
  </ul>
  <div class="note">
    <strong>Back up your data.</strong> No hosting service can guarantee against disk
    failure or operator error. Keep your own copies of anything you cannot afford to lose.
  </div>

  <h2>12. Changes to these terms</h2>
  <p>
    These terms may be updated when the law changes or the service does. Material changes
    will be announced in the panel in reasonable time before they take effect. If you do
    not accept a change, you may cancel the affected service before it applies. Continuing
    to use the service afterwards means you accept the new version.
  </p>

  <h2>13. Governing law and disputes</h2>
  <p>
    These terms are governed by the law of <%= governingLaw %>. If you are a consumer,
    this choice does not remove the protection of mandatory rules in the country where you
    live, and you may bring proceedings in your local courts.
  </p>
  <p>
    If the operator is not willing or obliged to take part in consumer arbitration
    proceedings, that is stated here: <strong><%= disputeStatement %></strong>.
  </p>
  <p>
    If any part of these terms turns out to be unenforceable, the rest continues to apply.
  </p>

  <h2>14. Contact</h2>
  <p>
    <% if (contactEmail) { %>
      Questions about these terms:
      <a href="mailto:<%= contactEmail %>"><%= contactEmail %></a>.
    <% } else { %>
      Questions about these terms should be addressed to the operator of this panel.
    <% } %>
  </p>

  <footer>
    <a href="/login">&larr; Back to sign in</a>
    <a href="/privacy">Privacy Policy</a>
    <span><%= siteName %> <%= panelVersion %></span>
  </footer>

</div>
</body>
</html>
